agent_3.revised_policy:
No individual may harvest more than 12% of the lake’s current stock in a single trip. Each trip begins with a communal tally of lake stock. Every fisher declares their intended catch; after the trip, a shared ledger records the actual catch. The steward verifies that declared catches do not exceed 12% of the stock at departure. If a fisher exceeds the limit, the excess must be returned to the lake or deposited into a community pool for later redistribution. Trips occur weekly, and the steward is selected by community vote at the start of each week and rotated thereafter. Failure to return excess triggers a communal review; the fisher may be asked to reduce their next trip’s quota or provide community service. The community records the lake’s current stock in a communal ledger at the start of each trip. Each fisher writes down their intended catch; the steward records these intentions. After the trip, each fisher deposits their catch into the shared ledger. The steward compares each fisher’s actual catch against their declared catch and against the 12% limit of the initial stock. If a fisher’s actual catch exceeds their declaration and the limit, the excess is identified. The fisher must return the excess to the lake or deposit it into the community pool within 24 hours. If the fisher does not do so, the steward initiates a communal review within two days, following the procedure outlined in the answer. All decisions are made by simple majority of the review committee. Excess fish deposited into the community pool shall be redistributed on a monthly basis according to the following criteria:
1) Priority to households with documented need. “Documented need” is defined by the community council as households that, through a monthly Need Declaration Form, have:
   a) Annual income below the community-defined poverty threshold;
   b) One or more children under 12;
   c) One or more elderly members (60+);
   d) One or more disabled members; or
   e) A medical condition requiring regular care (verified by a note from a community clinic).
2) A portion to community projects such as school lunch or health clinic.
3) Any remaining fish split equally among all households.
The allocation will be decided by simple majority vote at the monthly community meeting.
